          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "FASCINATING FACTS"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.112,
            0.457,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The world’s largest stone sundial, the Samrat Yantra, was built around 300 years ago at the Jantar Mantar, in Jaipur, Rajasthan, a UNESCO World Heritage site that houses several astronomical instruments. With its imposing height of 27 metres, its shadow moves at about 1 millimetre per"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.465,
            0.103,
            0.798,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.465,
                0.103,
                0.798,
                0.247
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.263,
            0.786,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "second and falls on a scale finely marked to measure time intervals as short as 2 seconds. Like any sundial, it measures local or ‘solar time’, requiring a correction to determine Indian Standard Time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.789,
            0.204,
            0.897,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.789,
                0.204,
                0.897,
                0.328
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.334,
            0.494,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Should we make a simple water clock?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.367,
            0.423,
            0.387
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 8.1: Let us construct",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.408,
            0.743,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a used transparent plastic bottle (1/2 litre or larger) with its cap."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.451,
            0.729,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cut it into two, roughly in the middle as shown in Fig. 8.5a."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.475,
            0.743,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using a drawing pin, make a small hole in the cap of the bottle (Fig. 8.5b)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
